Test: relaxing the max_concurrent check in compute_rsc_project_reason()

This was tested on top of the per-app mc_max_could_use patch, on BOINC 8.2.13, Linux x86_64.

Setup: LHC@home, 125 usable CPUs, buffer 0.25 + 0.03 days = 24,192 s. In app_config.xml, ATLAS max_concurrent is 31 (3 threads per task), CMS is 8 (4 threads per task), and Theory has no limit. LHC@home currently has no server-side CMS limit per host.

Problem: with only the mc_max_could_use patch, the client still logged Not requesting tasks: don't need (CPU: max concurrent job limit) and sent 0-second requests while cores were idle. The block comes from:

cpp
if (rsc_pwf.last_mc_limit_reltime > gstate.work_buf_total()) {
    return RSC_REASON_MAX_CONCURRENT;
}

Eight running CMS VMs (about 9 h each) keep CMS at its limit past the end of the buffer. That blocks work fetch for every app in the project, including Theory, which has no limit.

Change tested: only block when the limits cap the whole resource:

cpp
if (rsc_pwf.last_mc_limit_reltime > gstate.work_buf_total()
    && rsc_pwf.mc_max_could_use < rwf.ninstances
) {
    return RSC_REASON_MAX_CONCURRENT;
}

Result: the client did start fetching. work_fetch_debug showed last MC limit time 28800 total buf 24192, followed by Requesting new tasks for CPU.

But the requests were far too large and never stopped:

MC shortfall 2249856 (≈ 26 days of CPU)
the host received about 196 tasks, nearly all CMS, within minutes

The shortfall shows why. 2,249,856 / 24,192 ≈ 93 idle threads, so the simulation counted only 32 threads in use: exactly 8 CMS × 4. The 31 running ATLAS tasks (93 threads) weren’t counted. In RR_SIM::pick_jobs_to_run(), reaching CONCURRENT_LIMIT_APP sets at_max_concurrent_limit and removes the whole project from the rest of that pass, so the project’s other apps aren’t simulated.

Queued CMS tasks can’t start because of the limit, so they never reduce the shortfall. The client kept asking, and with no server-side CMS limit, the server kept sending CMS.

In the stock client, these two bugs cancel out. mc_max_could_use = smallest limit × largest task size = 8 × 4 = 32, and 32 − 32 gives a shortfall of 0.

Conclusion: I reverted this change. The last_mc_limit_reltime check is too broad, but it’s the only thing that stops work fetch for a project whose capped app already has a backlog. Relaxing it is only safe once the simulation counts the other apps correctly. A fix for that (in rr_sim.cpp) is being tested, and results will follow.

